<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <key>Label</key><string>__BUNDLE_ID__.helper</string>
  <key>BundleProgram</key><string>Contents/MacOS/__HELPER_NAME__</string>
  <!-- Woken on a schedule, so a mount whose server has gone does not sit
       there waiting for somebody to open a drive.

       macOS puts up "Server connections interrupted", naming the drive and
       offering Disconnect All, when an NFS mount stops answering. The app
       sweeps those away, and so does this helper -- but only while either is
       running, and this is an on-demand Mach service that launchd stops when
       nothing is talking to it. Measured on 2026-09-05: helper not running,
       a mount with nothing behind it still in the table three minutes after
       its engine was killed, and macOS had already said something.

       Sixty seconds, not five minutes. Five was written here first with a
       justification that does not hold: the worst case is a whole interval
       plus the minute the probe spends deciding a mount is dead rather than
       slow, so 300 would let one sit for six minutes -- past the point macOS
       begins to reckon, which is five request timeouts at sixty seconds each.
       At sixty the worst case is about two minutes.

       It costs almost nothing: the sweep reads the mount table and returns at
       once when nothing is served, which on an idle Mac is every time. -->
  <key>StartInterval</key><integer>60</integer>
  <key>MachServices</key>
  <dict><key>__BUNDLE_ID__.helper</key><true/></dict>
  <key>AssociatedBundleIdentifiers</key>
  <array><string>__BUNDLE_ID__</string></array>
</dict>
</plist>
